<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><!-- Original-type: ra--><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">11720472</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2375232</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">6692079</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1054/bjoc.2001.2079</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Regular Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Detection of MAGE-A3 in breast cancer patients&#x02019; sentinel lymph nodes</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Wascher</surname><given-names>R A</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Bostick</surname><given-names>P J</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Huynh</surname><given-names>K T</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Turner</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">3</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Qi</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">4</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Giuliano</surname><given-names>A E</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Hoon</surname><given-names>D S B</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="aff1"><label>1</label>Department of Molecular Oncology, <label>2</label>Joyce Eisenberg-Keefer Breast Center, <label>3</label>Department of Pathology, <label>4</label>Division of Biostatistics, John Wayne Cancer Institute, Saint John's Health Center, Santa Monica, CA, 90404</aff><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>01</day><month>09</month><year>2001</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>11</month><year>2001</year></pub-date><volume>85</volume><issue>9</issue><fpage>1340</fpage><lpage>1346</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>01</day><month>12</month><year>2000</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>16</day><month>07</month><year>2001</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>23</day><month>07</month><year>2001</year></date></history><copyright-statement>Copyright 2001, Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2001</copyright-year><permissions><copyright-holder>Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>The detection of occult metastatic breast cancer cells by RT-PCR is limited by the poor specificity of most tumour mRNA markers. MAGE-A3 is a highly specific tumour mRNA marker that is not expressed in non-cancer cells. This study assesses MAGE-A3 mRNA as a molecular marker for the detection of tumour cells in the sentinel lymph nodes (SLN) of breast cancer patients. Serial frozen sections of SLN (<italic>n</italic>= 121) were obtained from 77 AJCC (American Joint Committee on Cancer) Stage I&#x02013;IIIA breast cancer patients. MAGE-A3 mRNA analysis of SLN was performed by RT-PCR and Southern blot analysis. Tumour cells were detected in 48 of 121 (40&#x00025;) SLN from 77 patients by H&#x00026;E or IHC staining, and 35 of 77 (45&#x00025;) patients, overall, had histopathologically (H&#x00026;E and/or IHC) positive SLN. Among histopathologically negative SLN, 28 of 73 (38&#x00025;) SLN were MAGE-A3 mRNA positive by RT-PCR. Overall, 41 of 77 (53&#x00025;) patients and 50 of 121 (41&#x00025;) SLN were positive for MAGE-A3. MAGE-A3 mRNA expression in the SLN occurred more frequently with infiltrating lobular carcinoma (<italic>P</italic>&#x0003c; 0.001) than with infiltrating ductal carcinoma, adding further evidence of possible phenotypic differences between these 2 subtypes of breast cancer. Due to its high specificity, MAGE-A3 mRNA is a potentially useful marker for detecting breast cancer cells in the SLN. One half of breast tumours expressed MAGE-A3 mRNA, which has important potential implications for antigen-specific targeted immunotherapy. &#x000a9; 2001 Cancer Research Campaign</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>breast cancer</kwd><kwd>MAGE-A3</kwd><kwd>micrometastases</kwd><kwd>RT-PCR</kwd><kwd>sentinel lymph node</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>